 "story": {
  "kicker": "Central Business District",
  "h2": "Don Leoncio Cigars Bar",
  "paragraphs": [
   "Don Leoncio sits on Canal Street behind a neon sign that says, simply, CIGAR BAR. Inside it is one long room: a humidor wall, a counter where the cigars get rolled, a proper back bar with bottles stacked to the ceiling, then leather recliners and chesterfields going back into the dark. A smoke shop and a lounge at the same time, which on Canal Street is not a common arrangement.",
   "The leaf is Dominican, Honduran and Nicaraguan, and the boxes sit open on a table so you can look before you commit to anything. The bar pours whiskey, which is what most people set beside a cigar here, and there is Presidente in the cooler for the other way of going about it. There is a kitchen as well, and the plate that turns up most often in the photos is fried shrimp and fries."
  ]
 },
 "facts": [
  "Hand-rolled cigars: Dominican, Honduran and Nicaraguan leaf",
